2)$, whose volume is Catalan's constant. ... The proof combines Andreev's theorem, the Schläfli formula, Atkinson's decomposition into atoroidal and prismatic parts, explicit volume estimates for ordinary prisms and complete orthoschemes, and a direct equiangular version of Inoue's edge surgery.\nTitle: Minimal Equiangular Hyperbolic Polyhedra in the Tetrahedral Range\nAuthors: Andrey Egorov\nDate: Fri Aug 14 11:53:08 2026\n\nWe consider finite-volume convex hyperbolic polyhedra whose dihedral angles are all equal to a fixed number $α$. Such non-obtuse equiangular polyhedra may exist only for $\\fracπ{3}\\leqα\\leq\\fracπ{2}$. The endpoint cases of the minimal-volume problem are known: for $α=\\fracπ{3}$, the minimum is attained by the ideal regular tetrahedron, while for $α=\\fracπ{2}$, among right-angled polyhedra, it is attained by the triangular bipyramid $P(3,2)$, whose volume is Catalan's constant. We prove the corresponding statement in the tetrahedral range $\\fracπ{3}\\leqα<\\arccos\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$, where the regular hyperbolic tetrahedron with dihedral angle $α$ exists. Namely, for every such $α$, among all equiangular hyperbolic polyhedra with dihedral angle $α$, the minimum volume is attained only by this tetrahedron. The proof combines Andreev's theorem, the Schläfli formula, Atkinson's decomposition into atoroidal and prismatic parts, explicit volume estimates for ordinary prisms and complete orthoschemes, and a direct equiangular version of Inoue's edge surgery.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKapovich–Nagnibeda–Lim minimal volume entropy conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/408731/kapovich-nagnibeda-lim-minimal-volume-entropy-conjecture)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; # Kapovich–Nagnibeda–Lim minimal volume entropy conjecture ... geometric-group-theory metric-geometry riemannian-geometry ... Current status (as of October 2026): Related geometric progress has been claimed, but the Kapovich–Nagnibeda–Lim minimal volume entropy conjecture remains open.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Refreshed 1 week ago\n\nOpen · progress reported\n\nA new paper gives stronger geometric estimates relevant to the conjecture, but does not prove the conjecture, which remains open.\n\nThe Kapovich–Nagnibeda–Lim conjecture concerns minimizing volume entropy in the relevant geometric setting. The retrieved literature includes related entropy-minimization theorems, but no proof or counterexample for the specifically named conjecture.\n\nKnown results\n\nFor free groups of rank $k \\ge 2$k≥2, the minimum among volume-one metric graphs without degree-one or degree-two vertices is $(3k-3)\\log 2$(3 k−3)lo g 2, attained exactly by regular trivalent graphs with equal edge lengths; the entropy supremum is infinite. This is related work, not a stated resolution of the named conjecture.\n\nOctober 2026 all-scale bounds\n\nSteve Karam’s paper replaces an earlier lossy estimate with an all-radius comparison for universal covers of short metric graphs, improves an exponential-growth application to small-area surfaces, and gives explicit constants relevant to Guth’s question. Its abstract proposes a sharp all-scale conjecture rather than proving the full minimal-entropy conjecture; the advance is therefore unverified as a resolution.\n\nCurrent status (as of October 2026): Related geometric progress has been claimed, but the Kapovich–Nagnibeda–Lim minimal volume entropy conjecture remains open.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGhost series and a motivated proof of the Bressoud-Göllnitz-Gordon identities (https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01992)\nciteturn4academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.0 years ago; Title: Ghost series and a motivated proof of the Bressoud-Göllnitz-Gordon identities ... Date: Fri Nov  3 15:44:20 2023\nTitle: Ghost series and a motivated proof of the Bressoud-Göllnitz-Gordon identities\nAuthors: John Layne, Samuel Marshall, Christopher Sadowski, Emily Shambaugh\nDate: Fri Nov  3 15:44:20 2023\n\nWe present what we call a \"motivated proof\" of the Bressoud-G\\\"ollnitz-Gordon partition identities. Similar \"motivated proofs\" have been given by Andrews and Baxter for the Rogers-Ramanujan identities and by Lepowsky and Zhu for Gordon's identities. Additionally, \"motivated proofs\" have also been given for the Andrews-Bressoud partition identities by Kanade, Lepowsky, Russell, and Sills and for the G\\\"ollnitz-Gordon-Andrews identities by Coulson, Kanade, Lepowsky, McRae, Qi, Russell, and the third author. Our proof borrows both the use of \"ghost series\" from the \"motivated proof\" of the Andrews-Bressoud identities and uses recursions similar to those found in the \"motivated proof\" of the G\\\"ollnitz-Gordon-Andrews identities. We anticipate that this \"motivated proof\" of the Bressoud-G\\\"ollnitz-Gordon identities will illuminate certain twisted vertex-algebraic constructions.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nD.G.L. WANG AND TONGYUAN ZHAO (https://plouffe.fr/OEIS/citations/2009.05973.pdf)\nciteturn4search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.1 years ago; Bernardi, Duplantier and Nadeau [4] proved that the number of ballot permutations of length n equals the number of odd order permutations of length n, by using compositions of bijections, and thus Theorem 1.1 follows. ... The first author and Zhang [32] refined Spiros conjecture by tracking the neighbors of the largest letter in these permutations, which is still open.\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((-1)!! = 1\\).\n\nThe sequence \\(\\{b_n\\}_{n\\ge 0}\\) can be found in OEIS [28, A000246], of which the exponential generating function is\n\n(1.1) \\[\n\\sum_{n\\ge 0} b_n \\frac{x^n}{n!} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{1-x}}.\n\\]\n\nA ballot permutation whose corresponding ballot path ends on the line \\(y=x\\) is said to be a Dyck permutation, whose enumeration is the Eulerian-Catalan number, see Bdkhori and Sullivant [6].\n\nSpiro [29] introduced a statistic \\(M(\\pi)\\) for odd order permutations, and conjectured that the number of ballot permutations of length \\(n\\) with \\(d\\) descents equals the number of odd order permutations \\(\\pi\\) of length \\(n\\) such that \\(M(\\pi)=d\\). In this paper, we confirm the conjecture by computing their bivariate generating functions in terms of the Eulerian numbers, respectively.\n\n**Theorem 1.2.** *Let \\(n \\ge 1\\) and \\(0 \\le d \\le \\lfloor (n-1)/2 \\rfloor\\). The number of ballot permutations of length \\(n\\) with \\(d\\) descents equals the number of odd order permutations \\(\\pi\\) of length \\(n\\) with \\(M(\\pi)=d\\).*\n\nThe first author and Zhang [32] refined Spiros conjecture by tracking the neighbors of the largest letter in these permutations, which is still open. They defined a word \\(u\\) as a factor of a word \\(w\\) if there exist words \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) such that \\(w=xuy\\), and a word \\(u\\) as a cyclic factor of a permutation \\(\\pi \\in S_n\\) if \\(u\\) is a factor of some word \\(v\\) such that \\((v)\\) is a cycle of \\(\\pi\\). The conjecture is as follows.\n\n**Conjecture 1.3** (Wang and Zhang). *For all \\(n, d,\\) and \\(2 \\le j \\le n-1\\), we have*\n\\[\nb_{n,d}(1,j) + b_{n,d}(j,1) = 2p_{n,d}(1,j),\n\\]\n*where \\(b_{n,d}(i,j)\\) is the number of ballot permutations of length \\(n\\) with \\(d\\) descents which have \\(ij\\) as a factor, and \\(p_{n,d}(i,j)\\) is the number of odd order permutations of length \\(n\\) with \\(M(\\pi)=d\\) which have \\(ij\\) as a cyclic factor.*--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBallot Permutations and Odd Order Permutations (https://mathweb.ucsd.edu/~sspiro/Research/Slides/OOP.pdf)\nciteturn4search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.5 years ago; Their proof does not give a bijection between P(n) and B(n). Motivated by this, we will try and consider a refined versi